#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175687 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176040 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39593326-76e2-578f-af68-57f59712c30e" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Bernoulli Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "d15d6003-a4dd-599f-987d-289508968ac7" :question/math-academy-id "e-10105" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,\\ldots,X_{10}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i\\sim \\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta)$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta.$ For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{10},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{10} x_i = 7.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Bernoulli random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\theta^x (1-\\theta)^{1-x}, \\qquad x = 0,1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1=x_1, X_2=x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=10$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\theta^x(1-\\theta)^{1-x}, \\qquad x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =({\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ({\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{2}})\\,⋯\\,({\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (1-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i.$ \n\nWe are told that $\\displaystyle S=\\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i=7$ and $n=10.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{7}(1-\\theta )^{10-7} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{7}(1-\\theta )^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175708 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176062 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a2eb2ca-a19a-591e-810a-c028a17be252" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Binomial Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "3e3ca456-a46a-5a5b-9cf6-07caad631ff9" :question/math-academy-id "e-11117" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{12}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i \\sim B(4,\\theta)$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta.$ For a particular sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_{12},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\sum_{i=1}^{12} x_i = 22.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample up to a constant $C.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x) = \\displaystyle \\binom{n}{x} \\theta^x (1-\\theta)^{n-x}, \\qquad x = 0,1,2,\\ldots, n.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = P(X_1 = x_1, X_2 = x_2, \\ldots , X_n = x_n) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i;\\,\\theta).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n = 12$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta$ and $N = 4$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{4-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2,3,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (4-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,4n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{4n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle C = \\prod\\limits_{i=1}^n  {4 \\choose x_i}$ is a constant and $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^{n} x_i.$\n\nWe are told that $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^{n} x_i = 22$ and $n = 12.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{22}(1-\\theta )^{4(12)-22} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{22}(1-\\theta )^{26}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175709 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176063 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03d91774-9a9f-55a8-8a9a-090788318a98" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Poisson Random Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "9f08cf84-3e3b-5c86-adb6-622bc51a15f6" :question/math-academy-id "e-11118" :question/problem "Suppose $X_1,X_2,X_3,X_4,X_{5}$ is an I.I.D random sample with $X_i\\sim \\mathrm{Po}(\\theta)$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta.$ Find the likelihood function of the sample $0, 2, 3, 1, 1.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x; \\theta),$ where $\\theta$ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample $x_1, x_2, \\ldots, x_n$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta) = \\displaystyle\\prod_{i=1}^n f(x_i; \\theta).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=5$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x; \\theta) = \\dfrac{\\theta^{x} e^{-\\theta}}{x!}, \\qquad x=0,1,2,\\ldots\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!})\\cdot (\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!})⋯(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\displaystyle C=\\prod\\limits_{i=1}^{n} (x_i)!$ is a constant and $\\displaystyle S = \\sum\\limits_ {i=1}^n x_i.$\n\nFor our sample, we have that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =0+2+3+1+1 \\\\  & =7 \\\\ C & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})! \\\\  & =0!\\cdot 2!\\cdot 3!\\cdot 1!\\cdot 1! \\\\  & =1\\cdot 2\\cdot 6\\cdot 1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nand $n=5.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{12}\\,{\\theta }^{7}\\,{e}^{-5\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176372 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176063 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03d91774-9a9f-55a8-8a9a-090788318a98" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Poisson Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "638f95eb-fe03-5c9c-b325-ab64d1a2bfb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,{\\theta }^{18}\\,{e}^{-2\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435566} {:answer/id #uuid "838c6fad-c2ba-572a-946c-b271606a25e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12\\,{\\theta }^{16}\\,{e}^{-18\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435572} {:answer/id #uuid "c60c1729-d011-5a55-ab06-3140f2535dae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,{\\theta }^{2}\\,{e}^{-18\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435580} {:answer/id #uuid "da146878-2071-56de-b976-f02f6e1c86b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\,{\\theta }^{2}\\,{e}^{-16\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435581} {:answer/id #uuid "e63ce25c-9077-5101-b2f1-edcd452a4672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\,{\\theta }^{18}\\,{e}^{-16\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e63ce25c-9077-5101-b2f1-edcd452a4672" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}\\,{\\theta }^{18}\\,{e}^{-16\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435585} :answer-field/id #uuid "d1803602-4fb6-5082-a851-eb54e4e8a4d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186174490}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab6f3ebb-34cb-52f6-a546-298076d55271" :question/math-academy-id "q-143472" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{16}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{16},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{16}{x}_{i}=18,\\,{\\prod }_{i=1}^{16}({x}_{i})!=6.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=16$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!})\\cdot (\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!})⋯(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=18,$ $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!=6,$ and $n=16.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{6}\\,{\\theta }^{18}\\,{e}^{-16\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435597 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176040 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39593326-76e2-578f-af68-57f59712c30e" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Bernoulli Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{