e sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Bernoulli random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=12$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =({\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ({\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{2}})\\,⋯\\,({\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (1-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=7$ and $n=12.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{7}(1-\\theta )^{12-7} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{7}(1-\\theta )^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435595 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176040 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "39593326-76e2-578f-af68-57f59712c30e" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Bernoulli Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c1b5d67-2015-546a-b753-e86796910bb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{5}(1-\\theta )^{10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435559} {:answer/id #uuid "733053ef-496c-512e-b472-26d977bfe224" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{15}(1-\\theta )^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435569} {:answer/id #uuid "c09dcbe2-1249-5a2a-a814-42f3694504c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{10}(1-\\theta )^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435578} {:answer/id #uuid "c364aca3-b3e0-5c08-8833-f92f2c90e923" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{10}(1-\\theta )^{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435579} {:answer/id #uuid "e346e105-c8a1-5024-ace6-a389edb0c7db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{5}(1-\\theta )^{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435584}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c09dcbe2-1249-5a2a-a814-42f3694504c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\theta }^{10}(1-\\theta )^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435578} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f604128-dde5-5878-8593-fd5ec0ff3efc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "652b7852-9baf-5786-af1c-6a9b4e529ff9" :question/math-academy-id "q-159814" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta )$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{15},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{15}{x}_{i}=10.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Bernoulli random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=15$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =({\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ({\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{2}})\\,⋯\\,({\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (1-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$ \n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=10$ and $n=15.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\theta }^{10}(1-\\theta )^{15-10} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{10}(1-\\theta )^{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435594 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176062 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a2eb2ca-a19a-591e-810a-c028a17be252" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Binomial Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29052ac4-071e-54fb-ae16-82067d8037d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{24}(1-\\theta )^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435560} {:answer/id #uuid "4b5112df-b8a5-508d-bd8d-84e15497dc00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{8}(1-\\theta )^{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435564} {:answer/id #uuid "72aacfd9-f2cf-5f0b-b080-dad3ce66cc66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{24}(1-\\theta )^{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435568} {:answer/id #uuid "a69f56a5-8274-5d79-86ae-2334c516061c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{16}(1-\\theta )^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435576} {:answer/id #uuid "b25bf37a-582a-55f4-ac43-30454c204ab3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{16}(1-\\theta )^{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a69f56a5-8274-5d79-86ae-2334c516061c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{16}(1-\\theta )^{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435576} :answer-field/id #uuid "923e314c-9d30-5ad4-a1e8-be7e907b3953" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435593}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "43e2161d-2ae7-5d04-bc29-2934b939fe8c" :question/math-academy-id "q-159817" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{8}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼B(3,\\theta )$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{8},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{8}{x}_{i}=16.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample up to a constant $C.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{n-x},\\,x=0,1,2,…,n.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=8$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=3$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{3-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2,3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{3-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (3-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,3n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{3n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{3}{{x}_{i}})$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=16$ and $n=8.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{16}(1-\\theta )^{3(8)-16} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{16}(1-\\theta )^{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435598 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176062 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2a2eb2ca-a19a-591e-810a-c028a17be252" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Binomial Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "389582af-a58f-54a7-ae30-7b904bf70c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{84}(1-\\theta )^{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435561} {:answer/id #uuid "410f39b6-a584-5d06-bfad-158d3f61cf1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{24}(1-\\theta )^{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435562} {:answer/id #uuid "71541f16-f169-5d86-b0ba-8063949a0c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{24}(1-\\theta )^{84}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435567} {:answer/id #uuid "74f9697a-0180-5e52-8045-a99af5573240" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{84}(1-\\theta )^{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435570} {:answer/id #uuid "dd08ec01-50e9-52c6-8a79-0d3ad5f4d4fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{60}(1-\\theta )^{24}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "410f39b6-a584-5d06-bfad-158d3f61cf1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "C\\,{\\theta }^{24}(1-\\theta )^{60}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435562} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b2cde9e-b33c-5092-8ba3-4c4a250a40a0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435591}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cec4340f-d49e-5a8e-a40d-7d35274dd0d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-159818" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{14}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼B(6,\\theta )$ and unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{14},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{14}{x}_{i}=24.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample up to a constant $C.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{n}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{n-x},\\,x=0,1,2,…,n.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )=P({X}_{1}={x}_{1},{X}_{2}={x}_{2},…,{X}_{n}={x}_{n})={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\,\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=14$ independent binomial random variables with unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=6$ trials, for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x;\\theta )=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{6-x}, & \\,x=0,1,2,…,6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{6-{x}_{i}} \\\\  & =((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{1}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}(1-\\theta )^{6-{x}_{1}})\\cdot ((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{2}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}(1-\\theta )^{6-{x}_{2}})⋯((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{n}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}(1-\\theta )^{6-{x}_{n}}) \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,\\sum (6-{x}_{i})} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{\\,\\sum {x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{\\,6n-\\sum {x}_{i}} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{S}(1-\\theta )^{6n-S}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{6}{{x}_{i}})$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=24$ and $n=14.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =C\\,{\\theta }^{24}(1-\\theta )^{6(14)-24} \\\\  & =C\\,{\\theta }^{24}(1-\\theta )^{60}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186435596 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176063 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "03d91774-9a9f-55a8-8a9a-090788318a98" :knowledge-point/title "Likelihood Functions for Poisson Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43ce76c4-9b3f-5e0d-8d4b-7dd6c7eb8a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\,{\\theta }^{8}\\,{e}^{-2\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435563} {:answer/id #uuid "8a6185d9-5090-58f9-9faf-f0ca13d18ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8\\,{\\theta }^{17}\\,{e}^{-8\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435573} {:answer/id #uuid "a64a48b1-2e38-5fa2-80c1-00ca67502114" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,{\\theta }^{2}\\,{e}^{-17\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435575} {:answer/id #uuid "deae0969-c9d6-5654-be64-6233a00a8a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,{\\theta }^{8}\\,{e}^{-17\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435583} {:answer/id #uuid "f9f696ee-8ecd-5fda-b688-f8cccd8853c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\,{\\theta }^{2}\\,{e}^{-8\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435588}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "deae0969-c9d6-5654-be64-6233a00a8a9b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}\\,{\\theta }^{8}\\,{e}^{-17\\theta }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435583} :answer-field/id #uuid "75347981-9a28-5cb6-b027-b092523fc953" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186435590}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "83f5b615-5500-5815-8837-5144deae0691" :question/math-academy-id "q-159827" :question/problem "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{17}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼Po(\\theta )$ and unknown rate parameter $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{17},$ you're given that \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{17}{x}_{i}=8,\\,{\\prod }_{i=1}^{17}({x}_{i})!=4.\n$$\n\nFind the likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Poisson distribution is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\n$$\n\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\nL(\\theta )={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=17$ independent Poisson random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta ,$ for which the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )=\\frac{{\\theta }^{x}{e}^{-\\theta }}{x!},\\,x=0,1,2,…\\,.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{i}!} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{1}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{1}!})\\cdot (\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{2}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{2}!})⋯(\\frac{{\\theta }^{{x}_{n}}{e}^{-\\theta }}{{x}_{n}!}) \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-\\sum \\theta }\\cdot {\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}\\frac{1}{({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & ={\\theta }^{\\sum {x}_{i}}\\cdot {e}^{-n\\theta }\\cdot \\frac{1}{{\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{C}\\,{\\theta }^{S}\\,{e}^{-n\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!$ is a constant and $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nWe are told that $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}=8,$ $C={\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i})!=4,$ and $n=17.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(\\theta ) & =\\frac{1}{4}\\,{\\theta }^{8}\\,{e}^{-17\\theta }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
